---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageShell from '../components/site/PageShell.astro'
---

<Base title="About · R3F v10 Astro Starter">
  <PageShell
    eyebrow="about"
    title="One canvas, every route"
    lede="The same scene as the Vite and Next starters. What Astro adds is that the canvas can outlive the page it started on — and that everything around it ships no JavaScript at all."
  >
    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>client:only="react"</h2>
      <p>
        The canvas has no meaningful server render, and <code>three/webgpu</code> reaches for browser
        globals at import time — so the island is <code>client:only</code>, not
        <code>client:load</code>. Astro never tries to prerender it; the placeholder is empty markup
        until React hydrates on the client.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>transition:persist</h2>
      <p>
        Mounted in <code>Base.astro</code> with <code>transition:persist</code>, the island's DOM
        node is moved into the next page rather than recreated. Hover state, click accents, camera
        position and the Leva panel all survive navigation. It needs
        <code>&lt;ClientRouter /&gt;</code> in the head — that is the whole setup. The Next port
        needs <code>tunnel-rat</code> and a canvas hoisted into the root layout for the same effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not every site wants that. <code>src/config.ts</code> has a
        <code>CANVAS_MODE</code> switch: flip it to <code>'home-fixed'</code> and the canvas is
        mounted by <code>index.astro</code> instead, with no <code>transition:persist</code> — so
        leaving <code>/</code> unmounts it and disposes the renderer. Both paths are wired up; the
        diff between them is two lines.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>Zero-JS everywhere else</h2>
      <p>
        The nav is a plain <code>.astro</code> component and gets its active state from
        <code>Astro.url.pathname</code> at build time — no client component, no
        <code>usePathname</code>. The footer is a React component rendered <em>without</em> a client
        directive, so Astro compiles it to static HTML and ships none of its JavaScript. React is
        loaded for exactly one thing on this site: the canvas.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section">
      <h2>Sizing</h2>
      <p>
        R3F's own container is <code>width: 100%; height: 100%</code>, so it needs a sized parent. As
        a bare child of <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> it collapses to zero height and the renderer never
        boots — with no error. The fixed wrapper lives inside
        <code>Experience.tsx</code> so it travels with the island when Astro moves it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the <a href="/notes">notes</a> for the alpha rough edges, or go back to
        <a href="/">the scene</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </PageShell>
</Base>
